|\n58-| 10 | Graph path-width | Kinnersley 1992 [13] | graph | itself | definition (Robertson & Seymour), p. 346 | reference | `Pathwidth.lean` | definition |\n59-| 11 | Edge separation | Lengauer 1981 [14] | graph | itself | [14] defines no edge-separation number related to `pw`: its edge game is min-cut linear arrangement (cutwidth, p. 468), its Def. 6 is modified cutwidth; neither is within ±1 (stars); its vertex game VSG is `vs` exactly | `cw`, `mcw` fail ±1 on 341, 738 of 1,644 | — | **misattributed and false** under every reading [14] supports |\n60-| 12 | Vertex separation | Kinnersley 1992 [13] | graph | itself | `vs = pw` (Thm 3.1); Lengauer's VSG `= vs` by reversal | 1,652 graphs, 0 fail | `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth` | confirmed |\n61-\n62-Summary: **six confirmed** (MOSP, gate matrix layout, one-dimensional logic,\n63:narrowness, node search with the edgeless exception, vertex separation), plus\n64-path-width as the reference definition; **one unsourced** at its citation but\n65-proved elsewhere (interval thickness); **two weaker than stated** (edge search,\n66-a band; split bandwidth, a sandwich), both of which still meet the literal ±1\n67-wording; **two false as stated** (PLA folding in its standard two-nets-per-track\n68-form; edge separation, which is also misattributed).\n69-\n70-## The census, problem by problem\n71-\n72-### 1. MOSP — [1] Yanasse 1997 (EJOR), [4] Fink & Voss 1999\n73-\n74-- **Definition.** [1] p. 455: \"We assume that a stack is opened for every new\n75-  panel type cut and it remains opened until the last piece of that panel type\n76-  is cut. We also assume that a completed stack can be removed only after a\n77-  pattern is completely cut, hence, the maximum number of stacks occurs just\n78-  after some pattern is cut and before any completed part stack is removed.\"\n79-  [4] §1.1.1 (preprint p. 2), with `C` an m × n matrix whose **rows are\n80-  patterns** and columns order types: \"an order j may be defined open at\n81-  position i₀ of the pattern sequence if (Σ_{i ≤ i₀} c_{π_i,j})(Σ_{i ≥ i₀}\n82-  c_{π_i,j}) > 0\". Linhares & Yanasse 2002, p. 1760, eqs. (1)–(2), with the\n83-  I × J matrix P whose **rows are piece types**: `q_ij = 1` iff some patterns\n84-  x, y containing piece i satisfy `π(x) ≤ j ≤ π(y)`, and `Z_MOSP(P) = min_π\n85-  max_j Σ_i q_ij`. The three agree up to transposition of the matrix.\n86-- **Input.** 0/1 matrix. **Graph.** The MOSP graph: piece types (customers),\n87-  adjacent iff some pattern contains both (Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5).\n88-- **Relation.** Neither [1] nor [4] relates MOSP to any graph parameter; [1]\n89-  relates it to tool switching (Props. 1–2). The relation `Z = pw + 1` is\n90-  Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5 (the MOSP graph) with L&Y 2002 Prop. 2 (MOSP = GMLP)\n91-  and Fellows & Langston 1989 Thm 7 (GMLP cost k ⇔ pathwidth k − 1), whose\n92-  sketch rests on F&L 1987 Lemma 4.1. Proved in Lean,\n93-  `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, hypothesis: at least one requirement\n94-  (with none, `Z = 0` while `pw + 1 = 1`).\n95-- **Status.** Confirmed. The Table 1 references are sources for the problem,\n96-  not for the equivalence.\n97-\n98-### 2. Gate matrix layout — [6] Möhring 1990, [8] Wing, Huang & Wang 1985\n99-\n100-- **Definition.** Möhring p. 18: an m × n net–gate matrix M, rows nets,\n101-  columns gates; for a gate permutation π the augmented matrix M^π fills in\n102-  every 0 between a row's leftmost and rightmost 1; \"Nets of the augmented\n103-  net-gate matrix may share the same row (called track) if they have no gate\n--\n278-  K_{3,3}: es = 5 = Z + 1).\n279-\n280:### 8. Narrowness — [11] Kornai & Tuza 1992\n281-\n282-- **Definition.** §2 (preprint p. 2): for an in-sequence `(v_1, …, v_n)`, \"In\n283-  the i-th step, put vertex v_i from the IM into the shack; move all v_j (j ≤\n284-  i) with no neighbors v_k, k > i, from the shack to the OM; then go to the\n285-  (i+1)-st step. The maximum number of vertices in the shack during this\n286:  process ... will be called the narrowness of the in-sequence\". \"Definition.\n287:  [TK] The narrowness ν(G) of a graph G = (V, E) is the minimum value of\n288-  ν(v_1, …, v_n) taken over all permutations.\" Prop. 2.1: in- and\n289-  out-sequences give the same value (reverse the sequence).\n290-- **Relation.** Prop. 3.1 (p. 3): \"For every graph G with at least one vertex,\n291-  ν(G) = π(G) + 1\", proved both ways from path decompositions.\n292-- **Note for item 04.** The maximum is reached just after an insertion, so\n293-  `ν(v_1, …, v_n) = 1 + max_i |{j < i : v_j has a neighbour v_k, k ≥ i}|`,\n294-  which is the vertex separation of the same sequence plus one; per sequence,\n295-  not only at the optimum. The empty graph (ν = 0) is excluded by the source.\n296-- **Status.** Confirmed, exact.\n297-\n298-### 9. Split bandwidth — [12] Fomin 1998\n299-\n300-- **Definition.** §3.2 (preprint p. 7): node splitting of v: partition its\n301-  neighbourhood into M and N (either may be empty), \"delete vertex v with all\n302-  incident edges, add new vertices u and w with edge (u, w), and make u\n303-  adjacent to all vertices of M and w to all vertices of N\". \"A graph Γ* is\n304-  said to be a split of Γ if Γ* can be obtained from Γ by a sequence of node\n305-  splittings. The split bandwidth of graph Γ, denoted by sb(Γ), is min{b(Γ*) :\n306-  Γ* is a split of Γ}.\" Bandwidth b as usual (§2). The paper considers \"only\n307-  connected graphs with at least two vertices\" (p. 1).\n308-- **Relation.** Thm 8 (p. 11): \"For any graph Γ, pw(Γ) ≤ sb(Γ) ≤ pw(Γ) + 1.\"\n309-  Upper bound: a path decomposition with all bags of size k + 1 gives a\n310-  numbering of interval bandwidth ≤ k + 1, and `sb = ib` (Thm 6). Lower bound:\n311-  Γ is a minor of any split, and `pw ≤ bandwidth`. Also Thm 3: `1/μ_m = ib`\n312-  (monotone helicopter search), Thm 2: `1/μ_1 = b`. Fomin's concluding\n313-  remarks leave open when `pw = sb`.\n314-- **Both values occur** (by hand; item 02 to check): K